[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Certificate validation (/docs/platform/pki/certificate-validation)\n\n\n\nThis guide brings together the checks needed when integrating a certificate consumer. You need the presented certificate, intermediates and roots that your application has deliberately chosen to trust. Lamassu publishes status; the consumer applies acceptance policy.\n\nBefore querying status [#before-querying-status]\n\nVerify these criteria together:\n\n| Check               | Required result                                                                    |\n| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Chain and signature | Valid chain to a root deliberately installed in the consumer.                      |\n| Dates               | Current time between `Not Before` and `Not After`, including the CAs in the chain. |\n| Identity            | Name, SAN or identifier matches the expected peer.                                 |\n| Purpose             | KU, EKU and constraints permit the specific operation.                             |\n| Revocation          | Signed, current evidence corresponding to the queried issuer and certificate.      |\n| Authorization       | The application permits that identity to perform the action.                       |\n\nThe [issuance walkthrough](/docs/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate) checks chain and purpose with OpenSSL. That check does not automatically query OCSP or download a CRL. Inventory presence or an OCSP `Good` response does not replace the other criteria.\n\nChoose a mechanism [#choose-a-mechanism]\n\n| Mechanism | When to use it                                           | What you must maintain                                                     |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| OCSP      | Status query for a specific certificate.                 | Responder access, signature verification and cache/freshness policy.       |\n| CRL       | Local checks for many certificates or offline operation. | Periodic download of an issuer-signed list and rejection of expired lists. |\n\nOCSP can also return a response reusable until `nextUpdate`; a CRL changes when a new version is published. Neither mechanism alone guarantees that a consumer learns a revocation at the moment it is saved.\n\nIf using both, define when fallback is allowed and what the client does when retrieval, signature or freshness checks fail, or the result is `Unknown`. Accepting an identity when its status cannot be checked must be an explicit application decision.\n\nCheck consumer behavior [#check-consumer-behavior]\n\n1. Accept a current, authorized certificate with a known chain.\n2. Reject another certificate with an unknown root or incompatible purpose.\n3. Revoke a test certificate and verify the OCSP response or new CRL.\n4. Check that a new connection with that identity is rejected.\n5. Simulate an unreachable endpoint and expired evidence; observe whether the result matches policy.\n\nKeep the serial number, issuer, revocation reason, query time and evidence timestamps. An existing session may need explicit closure or reauthentication in the consumer; publishing revocation does not automatically close it.\n\nFor a chain with intermediates, validate their status according to consumer policy as well. An end-entity issuer’s CRL does not replace checks on every CA in the chain.\n\n<Cards>\n  <Card title=\"Online validation with OCSP\" description=\"Generate a query and verify signature, status and timestamps.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/ocsp\" />\n\n  <Card title=\"Revocation lists\" description=\"Download, inspect and use an issuer’s CRL.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/crl\" />\n</Cards>\n"]
